Z||000012||william_thorniii||03-01-2001||06:43 PM||||No worries GM fans.  The car described in the article is the Lotus Elise/Opel Speedster/Vauxhaul Speedster.  Those of you lucky enough to attend the Detroit Int'l Auto Show should have seen a blue Vauxhaul Speedster in the GM exhibit.  Cliff you are correct, the drawing in the article is definetly of the Opel Tigra convertable.  I too saw many of them when I was going to University in France back in '98.  My opinions can be backed up by looking at the drawing and comparing it to the description.  Where exactly would that mid-mounted engine reside in the drawing?  I can't wait for 2010.  By then I might be able to afford one of these babies... of course if it passes through Pontiac design house... it will come out with all of those damn raked body side cladding... I've got two words for Pontiac styling, uh gly.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||13
Z||000013||87ECO5||03-02-2001||08:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>... of course if it passes through Pontiac design house... it will come out with all of those damn raked body side cladding... I've got two words for Pontiac styling, uh gly.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Amen, brother WT3!!!  Part of the beauty of that car is the clean styling.  Please, Pontiac, PLEASE!!!  If you build it, please don't clutter the styling with the typical tacked-on cladding!||||reg||8
